      "content": "GENERAL INFORMATION",
      "contentHtml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "a9b72752-c1e8-46ee-bf46-01946664bd0b",
      "type": "GeneralText",
      "area": "General",
      "position": 1,
      "content": "Major Specific Criteria This articulation agreement displays lower-division course requirements specific to the major. Cal State LA lower-division course numbers range from 1000 - 2999. Students are advised to seek advisement from a counselor when planning to transfer to CSU Los Angeles and refer to the University Catalog to learn more about this major and its features. Additional admissions criteria for the major can be found on the Major Specific Criteria campus website. Please note that admissions requirements can vary by term so check back to see if there are any changes.",
      "contentHtml": "<p><strong style=\"font-size: 24px; color: rgb(169, 62, 58);\">Major Specific Criteria</strong></p><p><br></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 16px;\">This articulation agreement displays lower-division course requirements specific to the major. Cal State LA lower-division course numbers range from 1000 - 2999. Students are advised to seek advisement from a counselor when planning to transfer to CSU Los Angeles and refer to the </span><a href=\"https://ecatalog.calstatela.edu/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"font-size: 16px;\">University Catalog</a><span style=\"font-size: 16px;\"> to learn more about this major and its features. Additional admissions criteria for the major can be found on the </span><a href=\"https://www.calstatela.edu/admissions/major-specific-criteria\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"font-size: 16px;\">Major Specific Criteria</a><span style=\"font-size: 16px;\"> campus website.&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"font-size: 16px; color: rgb(137, 58, 171);\"><em>Please note that admissions requirements can vary by term so check back to see if there are any changes.</em>&nbsp; </strong></p>"
    },
    {
      "id": "15cc6f45-6887-ef11-8474-000d3a37e3b6",
      "type": "GeneralTitle",
      "area": "General",
      "position": 2,
      "content": "RESOURCES FOR TRANSFER STUDENTS",
      "contentHtml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "1acc6f45-6887-ef11-8474-000d3a37e3b6",
      "type": "GeneralText",
      "area": "General",
      "position": 3,
      "content": "Cal State Apply Cal State LA College of Arts and Letters College of Arts and Letters Advisement Center Degree with a Guarantee Department of Modern Languages and Literatures General Education Lower Division Courses University Catalog",
      "contentHtml": "<p></p><p><a href=\"https://www.calstate.edu/apply\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"font-size: 16px;\">Cal&nbsp;State&nbsp;Apply</a></p><p><a href=\"http://www.calstatela.edu/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"font-size: 16px;\">Cal&nbsp;State&nbsp;LA</a><a href=\"https://ecatalog.calstatela.edu/preview_program.php?catoid=70&amp;poid=31599\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"font-size: 16px;\">&nbsp;</a></p><p><a href=\"http://www.calstatela.edu/academic/al\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"font-size: 16px;\">College&nbsp;of&nbsp;Arts&nbsp;and&nbsp;Letters</a></p><p><a href=\"http://www.calstatela.edu/academic/al/advisementfaq.php\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"font-size: 16px;\">College&nbsp;of&nbsp;Arts&nbsp;and&nbsp;Letters&nbsp;Advisement&nbsp;Center</a></p><p><a href=\"http://adegreewithaguarantee.com/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"font-size: 16px;\">Degree&nbsp;with&nbsp;a&nbsp;Guarantee</a></p><p><a href=\"http://www.calstatela.edu/academic/mld\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"font-size: 16px;\">Department&nbsp;of&nbsp;Modern&nbsp;Languages&nbsp;and&nbsp;Literatures</a></p><p><a href=\"https://ecatalog.calstatela.edu/content.php?catoid=77&amp;navoid=11897\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"font-size: 16px;\">General&nbsp;Education&nbsp;Lower&nbsp;Division&nbsp;Courses&nbsp;</a></p><p><a href=\"http://ecatalog.calstatela.edu/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"font-size: 16px;\">University&nbsp;Catalog</a></p>"
    },
    {
      "id": "18cc6f45-6887-ef11-8474-000d3a37e3b6",
      "type": "GeneralTitle",
      "area": "General",
      "position": 4,
      "content": "DIVERSITY REQUIREMENT FOR CAL STATE LA GRADUATION",
      "contentHtml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "afdd7145-6887-ef11-8474-000d3a37e3b6",
      "type": "GeneralText",
      "area": "General",
      "position": 5,
      "content": "The articulation information in Assist.org not only shows a link to the admission requirements (aka our Major Specific Criteria webpage) but also Cal State LA graduation requirements that may be completed at the lower-division level, prior to transferring to the University. The diversity requirement is a graduation requirement , not an admission requirement. The diversity requirement may be completed prior to, or after, transferring to the University. For the Cal State LA degree, students will be required to complete two courses (six units) certified as diversity courses, at least one focusing on issues of race and ethnicity and their intersectionality with other social categories that structure inequality in society. These courses can be completed either at the lower-division or upper-division level from among courses satisfying GE requirements. Please view the General Education agreements on Assist.org for the lower-division courses that will meet the Diversity (d) and Race &amp; Ethnicity (re) Requirements.",
      "contentHtml": "<p><span style=\"font-size: 16px;\">The articulation information in Assist.org not only shows a link to the admission requirements (aka our </span><a href=\"http://www.calstatela.edu/admissions/major-specific-criteria\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"font-size: 16px;\">Major Specific Criteria </a><span style=\"font-size: 16px;\">webpage) but also Cal State LA graduation requirements that may be completed at the lower-division level, prior to transferring to the University. The diversity requirement is a </span><strong style=\"font-size: 16px; color: rgb(169, 62, 58);\">graduation requirement</strong><span style=\"font-size: 16px;\">, not an admission requirement. The diversity requirement may be completed prior to, or after, transferring to the University. </span></p><p><br></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 16px;\">For the Cal State LA degree, students will be required to </span><strong style=\"font-size: 16px; color: rgb(169, 62, 58);\">complete two courses (six units)</strong><span style=\"font-size: 16px;\"> certified as diversity courses, at least one focusing on issues of race and ethnicity and their intersectionality with other social categories that structure inequality in society. These courses can be completed either at the lower-division or upper-division level from among courses satisfying GE requirements.</span></p><p><br></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 16px;\">Please view the </span><strong style=\"font-size: 16px; color: rgb(169, 62, 58);\">General Education </strong><span style=\"font-size: 16px;\">agreements on Assist.org for the lower-division courses that will meet the </span><strong style=\"font-size: 16px;\">Diversity (d)</strong><span style=\"font-size: 16px;\"> and </span><strong style=\"font-size: 16px;\">Race &amp; Ethnicity (re)</strong><span style=\"font-size: 16px;\"> Requirements.</span></p>"
    },
    {
      "id": "1bcc6f45-6887-ef11-8474-000d3a37e3b6",
      "type": "GeneralTitle",
      "area": "General",
      "position": 6,
      "content": "Japanese, B.A.",
      "contentHtml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "12cc6f45-6887-ef11-8474-000d3a37e3b6",
      "type": "GeneralText",
      "area": "General",
      "position": 7,
      "content": "Overview The program in Japanese includes a major for the Bachelor of Arts degree and a minor. The program emphasizes the development of functional communicative skills in Japanese and offers an in-depth study of Japanese literature, language, and civilization. Acquiring functional skills in listening, speaking, reading, writing, and culture gives students direct access to the Japanese people and society, enabling them to better understand and interact with the people of Japan today. The study of Japanese literature, language, and civilization broadens intellectual horizons, fosters an appreciation of cultural and linguistic diversity, and helps equip students to take their place as global citizens of the twenty-first century. Requirements for the Degree The total number of units required for the Bachelor of Arts degree in Japanese is 120 units. A total of 37 units are required for the major, including at least 16 units earned in residence at Cal State L.A. Elementary and intermediate Japanese (JAPN 1001, JAPN 1011, JAPN 1002, JAPN 1012, JAPN 2001, and JAPN 2011) , or an equivalent level of proficiency, are prerequisites to the major. All Japanese major students complete 22 units of required core courses and 15 units of directed electives for a total of 37 units. Consult with an advisor for the specific number of units required in all areas of the degree, including GE and free electives. Program Contact: The Department of Modern Languages and Literatures, phone: (323) 343-4230.",
      "contentHtml": "<p><strong style=\"font-size: 16px;\">Overview</strong></p><p><br></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 16px;\">The program in Japanese includes a major for the Bachelor of Arts degree and a minor. The program emphasizes the development of functional communicative skills in Japanese and offers an in-depth study of Japanese literature, language, and civilization.</span></p><p><br></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 16px;\">Acquiring functional skills in listening, speaking, reading, writing, and culture gives students direct access to the Japanese people and society, enabling them to better understand and interact with the people of Japan today. The study of Japanese literature, language, and civilization broadens intellectual horizons, fosters an appreciation of cultural and linguistic diversity, and helps equip students to take their place as global citizens of the twenty-first century.</span></p><p><br></p><p><br></p><p><strong style=\"font-size: 16px;\">Requirements for the Degree</strong></p><p><br></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 16px;\">The total number of units required for the Bachelor of Arts degree in Japanese is 120 units. A total of 37 units are required for the major, including at least 16 units earned in residence at Cal State L.A. Elementary and intermediate Japanese</span><strong style=\"font-size: 16px; color: rgb(43, 134, 24);\"> (JAPN 1001, JAPN 1011, JAPN 1002, JAPN 1012, JAPN 2001, and JAPN 2011)</strong><span style=\"font-size: 16px;\">, or an equivalent level of proficiency, are prerequisites to the major.&nbsp;All Japanese major students complete 22 units of required core courses and 15 units of directed electives for a total of 37 units. Consult with an advisor for the specific number of units required in all areas of the degree, including GE and free electives.</span></p><p><br></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 16px;\">Program Contact: The Department of Modern Languages and Literatures, phone: (323) 343-4230.</span></p><p><br></p><p><br></p><p><br></p>"
    }
  ],
